<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish Corridor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish Corridor was a strip of territory along the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> that gave the restored Polish state access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It was transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> from the rest of Germany. Its loss was among the territorial settlements that followed the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-corridor.jpg" alt="Polish Corridor" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Space Cadet at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish_Corridor.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Polish Corridor, also called the Pomeranian Corridor, lay in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, or Eastern Pomerania, and gave the Second Polish Republic access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, dividing the bulk of Weimar Germany from the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. At its narrowest the Polish territory was only 30 kilometres wide. To its east lay the Free City of Danzig, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, a semi-independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> where German was spoken, under the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in customs, mail, foreign policy, railways and defence. The word corridor was regarded as offensive by interwar Polish diplomats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> referred to the region as Pomorze or the Pomeranian Voivodeship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> was settled in the tenth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomeranians-slavic-tribe">Slavic Pomeranians</a>, ancestors of the Kashubians, who were subdued by Bolesław I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its dukes ruled until 1294. From 1308-1309 it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, and in 1466, by the second Peace of Thorn, it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> as part of autonomous Royal Prussia. Prussia annexed it in 1772 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a>, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. The corridor was therefore not an entirely new creation. Wilson's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a> of January 1918 proposed an independent Polish state with free and secure access to the sea, and Roman Dmowski argued that Poland could never be truly independent without it.</p><p>The corridor roused great resentment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> government recognised the eastern borders set at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>. In 1922 Germany set up a ferry service to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> to reduce its dependence on transit through Polish territory, and trains passed through with sealed carriages. In 1925 the Polish land reform listed 10,800 hectares from 32 German landowners and 950 hectares from seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, though Germans owned 39 percent of the agricultural land. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1910 counted 528,000 Poles, including Kashubians, and 385,000 Germans in the region.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 1 September 1939. The German Fourth Army defeated the Polish Pomorze Army and captured the corridor in the Battle of Tuchola Forest by 5 September, and it was annexed by Germany until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took it at the end of the war. Poland's borders were reorganised at the 1945 Potsdam Conference, and the corridor became part of the reborn Polish state. Many German residents were executed and others expelled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish Corridor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its territory, however, bore most of Germany's losses under the Treaty of Versailles: Eupen-Malmedy, the Hultschiner Ländchen, Danzig, the Memel Territory and much of Posen and West Prussia, leaving East Prussia cut off by the <b>Polish Corridor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Versailles stripped Alsace-Lorraine, the territories that formed the <b>Polish Corridor</b>, and all overseas colonies, imposed massive reparations, and reduced the army to 100,000 men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
